/**

@page tutorials_exahype2_applications_exagrype_running Use ExaGRyPE

The build and running of the ExaGRyPE is wrapped by an python interface for convenience. By calling 
the python script in command terminal, one can easily generate an executable that can run on any 
computational platform, from a students'laptop to supercomputer clusters. Different scenario can be 
picked by feeding different physics parameters into python script, the simulation setup and running parameter
can also be controled directly via command line.

Users are referred to \ref tutorials_exahype2_hitchhikers-guide if they are interested in what is going on in
the python script, which is also self-explaining most of time. 

# Gauge Wave

The gauge wave sceranio represent a dynamic foliation of a static Minkowski spacetime, it serves
a good benchmark setup for users (and also developers) to test their implemenation of the code. The detailed
explanation and eqautions of this benchmark can be found in https://arxiv.org/abs/gr-qc/0305023, section IV.B.

To run a gauge wave simulation in ExaGRyPE, use the command 
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
python3 ccz4.py -impl fd4-rk1-adaptive -s gauge -maxh 0.05 -minh 0.05 -ps 9 -plt 0.1 -et 0.5 -exn test --domain_r 0.5 --ReSwi 0 -cfl 0.1 --KOSigma 8.0
export OMP_NUM_THREADS=1
mpirun -n 1 ./peano4_test
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Type parameter --help makes the Python script yield further info and an explanation of each parameter option. 
There are also some specific running parameter of ExaGRyPE which we do not recommend to change.

# Clean up old and temporary files

The following statements remove all the glue code and autogenerated C++ files.
There is no risk in deleting them - if you rerun the Python script, they will 
all be reinstantiated.

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
rm *.o *.cmake
rm README-*.md
rm Makefile CMakeLists.txt
rm Abstract*
rm -rf celldata facedata globaldata observers tasks vertexdata repositories
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

To get rid of the output files, type in

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
rm *patch-file *vtu *.pvd *.bak *.csv
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

# Visualisation

The output of ExaHyPE is given in peano patch file and one can use the following commands to 
convert them into normal vtu files, and inspect them in other tools like paraview.

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
module unload python/2.7.15
module load python/3.6.5 paraview/5.8.1
export PYTHONPATH=/path/to/Peano/python/
pvpython /path/to/Peano/python/peano4/visualisation/render.py path/to/solution.peano-patch-file 
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~




*/

